message: "If you use the Fractal Resonance Processor, please cite the software using these metadata."
title: "Fractal Resonance Processor (FRP): Ternary Fractal Resonant Coherence Processor"
abstract: >-
  Fractal Resonance Processor (FRP) is a Ternary Fractal Resonant Coherence
  Processor architecture in which computation is organized through retained
  relative-phase interference, resonant selection, multiscale phase coherence,
  deterministic scheduling, and balanced ternary retained-state execution in
  the -1/0/1 domain. State 0 is an active computational state that performs
  mediation, routing, retention, balancing, and controlled neutralization.
  Opposite-polarity execution follows -1 to 0 to 1 and 1 to 0 to -1, with
  target state and executed retained state kept distinct. FRP v3.3.0 publishes
  the qualified M31 phase-interference, active-zero, and thermal-evidence
  boundary over the preserved FRP v3.2.0 / M30 archival baseline. The M31
  publication contains four deterministic canonical outputs: formal schema,
  evidence record, publication manifest, and qualification record. Its two
  cycle-exact execution contours contain 100 execution records, 800 cell
  observations, 702 active-zero observations, five requested direct events
  that are all prevented and neutral-routed, and zero actual direct
  opposite-polarity events. The focused M31 qualification records 13 of 13
  checks and 60 of 60 tests passing. Historical and current thermal and
  benchmark evidence remain separate measurement contours, the immutable M30
  archive remains byte-identical, the M15 executable semantic reference and
  M16 RTL and FPGA implementation anchor remain preserved, and the one-way
  published-byte boundary to FRP Trace Observatory remains read-only.
